<http://w3id.org/sepses/resource/attack/tactic/TA0005> a attack:Tactic ;
    dcterm:created "2018-10-17T00:14:20.652000+00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterm:description """The adversary is trying to avoid being detected.

Defense Evasion consists of techniques that adversaries use to avoid detection throughout their compromise. Techniques used for defense evasion include uninstalling/disabling security software or obfuscating/encrypting data and scripts. Adversaries also leverage and abuse trusted processes to hide and masquerade their malware. Other tactics’ techniques are cross-listed here when those techniques include the added benefit of subverting defenses.""" ;
    dcterm:identifier "TA0005" ;
    dcterm:modified "2022-04-25T14:00:00.188000+00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterm:title "Defense Evasion" .

<http://w3id.org/sepses/resource/attack/technique/T1033> a attack:Technique ;
    dcterm:created "2017-05-31T21:30:35.733000+00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterm:description """Adversaries may attempt to identify the primary user, currently logged in user, set of users that commonly uses a system, or whether a user is actively using the system. They may do this, for example, by retrieving account usernames or by using [OS Credential Dumping](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1003). The information may be collected in a number of different ways using other Discovery techniques, because user and username details are prevalent throughout a system and include running process ownership, file/directory ownership, session information, and system logs. Adversaries may use the information from [System Owner/User Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1033) during automated discovery to shape follow-on behaviors, including whether or not the adversary fully infects the target and/or attempts specific actions.

Various utilities and commands may acquire this information, including <code>whoami</code>. In macOS and Linux, the currently logged in user can be identified with <code>w</code> and <code>who</code>. On macOS the <code>dscl . list /Users | grep -v '_'</code> command can also be used to enumerate user accounts. Environment variables, such as <code>%USERNAME%</code> and <code>$USER</code>, may also be used to access this information.

On network devices, [Network Device CLI](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/008) commands such as `show users` and `show ssh` can be used to display users currently logged into the device.""" ;
    dcterm:identifier "T1033" ;
    dcterm:modified "2023-09-29T19:50:06.736000+00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterm:title "System Owner/User Discovery" ;
    attack:accomplishesTactic <http://w3id.org/sepses/resource/attack/tactic/TA0007> ;
